{
  "contract": "push/v2",
  "about": "Shared fixture between portico-ha (sender) and portico-app (receiver); sender design §4, §8.2. Each case is a sequence of FCM data maps as delivered to the phone, the phone's clock (now_ms) when the LAST one arrives, and the expected reaction to that last message: ring (full-screen ring), silent (nothing), dismiss (stop ringing, 'Answered elsewhere'), missed (stop or skip the ring, 'Missed call'), quiet (one quiet notification, never a ring, no call state). RECORDED by portico-ha's tests/test_push_wire.py from the real builders; portico-app vendors this file with a pinned sha256 and runs it against its pure decision function. Any change is a contract version bump on both sides. push/v2 (2026-10-20, M6 E3) is v1.1 with pv \"2\", `door_ref` derived from the door's endpoint id (`d-` and 32 hex: never an entry id, never a URL, so a door whose id is a URL still fits the id grammar ^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,128}$), and two optional keys reserved for the house calls (M7): `ct`, the call type, `door` when absent or `house`, and `from_ref`, an opaque caller reference. The app checks `ct` BEFORE it requires `door_ref`: `ct: house` is ignored on 0.5.0 (Ignored(UNKNOWN_KIND), journalled, not rung). From M7 3c (v2.1, cases appended, no version change) the sender sends both keys for an away phone's house ring: ct \"house\", an opaque from_ref and NO door_ref; the v2.1 cases are silent on 0.5.0. kind \"test\" carries only pv, kind and t. A message with pv \"1\" is unknown and ignored.",
  "ring_window_s": 45,
  "clock_skew_ms": 15000,
